    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-51432",
      "knowledge_point_id": "51c15d7a-cd9c-58df-a750-97df0b695c64",
      "knowledge_point": "Determining Intervals of Concavity From the Graph of a Function",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041640/assets/0c8969fae3e21c8e5f5019509636e2307211921d742e6f067d9ee1c50499541b.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=f(x),$ defined on $[-3,3].$ Find the intervals on which the function $f(x)$ is concave up.",
      "worked_solution": "The concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($⌣$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($⌢$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nWe can see that the function is curved upwards like a smile ($⌣$) over the intervals $(-3,-1)$ and $(1,3).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041640/assets/ae9ce6ca2597a4d5d1ca150c1e97d7c7a0a35f59fe5d6421b75203c6882d2052.png)",
